"""        <h2>The Scene <span class="em">🎬</span></h2>
        <p class="lead">Every city's transport system has one conversation nobody practises — and everyone eventually has.</p>

        <p>You've lived here five months. You tapped, you paid, you sat down — and somewhere between the app's cheerful interface and the city's zone map, you bought a ticket to the wrong zone. You didn't know. That's true. It's also, painfully, what everyone says.</p>

        <p>Here's what's actually true about ticket inspectors: they meet every kind of passenger, and within ten seconds they've sorted you into one of two boxes. Box one: the dodger — no ticket, a story, a fight, headphones back in. Box two: the honest mistake — wrong zone, expired pass, a tourist upside-down in the app. The fine is mandatory for box one. For box two, most systems — and most inspectors — have discretion: the paid difference, the warning, the benefit of the doubt. Which box you land in is decided almost entirely by your first two sentences.</p>

        <p>The winning strategy is absurdly simple and quite hard under pressure: get there first. Show the ticket before it's demanded, name the mistake before it's discovered, offer to pay the difference before it's asked. The person who declares the problem is a citizen with a paperwork error. The person who waits to be caught is a fare dodger with an excuse. Same ticket, opposite endings.</p>

        <ol class="bigq">
            <li>Is a rule a rule, or should honest mistakes be treated differently from deliberate ones? Where's the line?</li>
            <li>Why does being caught feel shameful even when we've done nothing deliberate? What does that feeling make us do — and say?</li>
        </ol>

        <div class="whos">
            <div class="who"><b>You</b><span>Five months in the city, a wrong-zone ticket bought in good faith, a phone with the purchase history — and ninety seconds to be believed.</span></div>
            <div class="who"><b>Inspector Brandt</b><span>Fifteen years on the trams. Has heard every story twice. Carries a fine machine, a zone map, and — for the honest ones — discretion.</span></div>
        </div>

        <div class="tip"><b>First:</b> read the conversation and mark the moment You gets ahead of the discovery — the mistake named before the inspector finds it. Everything that goes well afterwards flows from that one early sentence.</div>""",

