"""        <h2>Useful Phrases <span class="em">🧰</span></h2>
        <p class="lead">Ten phrases for the moment you're in the wrong and an official is standing over you — inspectors, parking wardens, customs, security. Say each out loud BEFORE you reveal it.</p>

        <div class="deliver"><b>Say it like you mean it.</b> The register is calm ownership: no grovelling, no fighting, no story-spinning. You're a person who made a paperwork error and intends to fix it — every sentence should sound like that person, because officials extend discretion to exactly that person and no one else.</div>

        <h3>🏁 Getting there first</h3>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 1</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> declaring the problem before it's discovered. Complete it:<br><br>"Before you scan it — I think I've done something ___."</p>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"Before you scan it — I think I've done something wrong."</b><br>The single highest-value sentence in the whole encounter. Discovered problems belong to dodgers; declared problems belong to honest people — and the scanner was going to find it in four seconds anyway, so the confession costs nothing and buys the box you want to be in. The instinct under pressure is to go quiet and hope. Override it. Hope is not a zone ticket.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "Before you check — I think I've done something wrong." Customs, tax letters, the boss's audit. Get there first, always.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 2</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> owning it cleanly — no machine-blaming. Which version?</p>
            <div class="choice">
                <div><b>A.</b> "The machine must have glitched — it's honestly not my fault."</div>
                <div><b>B.</b> "I bought the wrong zone — that's my mistake, not the machine's."</div>
                <div><b>C.</b> "Everyone says these zones are impossible, it's a stupid system."</div>
            </div>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the answer</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>B — "I bought the wrong zone — that's my mistake, not the machine's."</b><br>A is the sentence the inspector hears forty times a day from people whose machines all mysteriously glitched — it's the dodger's uniform, and wearing it costs you the discretion. C attacks the system the inspector works for; bold strategy. B is so rare it stopped him mid-scan: "most people are telling me the machine was broken." Clean ownership is startling, and startling an official into looking at you properly is exactly the goal.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "That's my mistake, not the ___'s." The ownership that makes everything after it believable.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 3</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> context without whining. Complete it:<br><br>"I'm five months in this city and the zone map still defeats me. That's an ___, not an ___."</p>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"That's an explanation, not an excuse."</b><br>The line that lets you give context without sounding like you're dodging — because you've labelled it yourself, before the official can. An excuse asks to cancel the consequence; an explanation just helps the other person understand what kind of mistake this was. Officials need the second to exercise discretion, and resent the first. Say which one you're offering, out loud.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "That's an explanation, not an excuse." Late work, missed calls, wrong forms — context, correctly labelled.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 4</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> evidence instead of adjectives. Complete it:<br><br>"Here's the app history — I've bought the right zones every day for five months. You can ___."</p>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"Here's the app history — right zones every day for five months. You can scroll."</b><br>"I'm honest, I promise" is an adjective; five months of correct tickets is a fact — and facts are the only currency officials can actually accept, because "he seemed nice" doesn't survive their paperwork. "You can scroll" is the masterstroke: evidence offered for inspection, nothing to hide. It's also, as the inspector notes at the end, exactly what wins the appeal — shown early, it wins the tram instead.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "Here's the history — you can scroll." Bank disputes, insurance claims, any accusation your records can answer.</div>
        </div>

        <h3>💶 Fixing it on the spot</h3>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 5</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> separating yourself from the fare, not the consequence. Put the pieces in order:</p>
            <div class="jumble"><span>I'll pay the difference</span><span>I'm not trying to dodge the fare —</span><span>right now.</span></div>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"I'm not trying to dodge the fare — I'll pay the difference right now."</b><br>The offer that proves the ownership was real: money, moving towards the system, unprompted. It reframes the whole event from "caught" to "correcting" — and note the addition "plus whatever the rules say", which accepts the consequence instead of begging it away. People who offer to pay before they're made to are the entire reason discretion exists.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "I'm not trying to avoid it — I'll pay the difference now." Underpaid bills, undercharged shops, honest messes of every kind.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 6</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> asking about mercy without demanding it. Complete it:<br><br>"What are my ___ here? Is there a version of this that isn't the full ___?"</p>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"What are my options here? Is there a version of this that isn't the full fine?"</b><br>Almost every penalty system has a lower tier — the reduced rate, the warning, the caution — and almost nobody asks, because asking feels like pushing luck. But look at the form: it's a question about the SYSTEM ("what are my options"), not a plea about yourself, finished with respect for his knowledge ("you'd know better than me"). Demands close discretion; questions open it. The twelve-euro tier existed the whole time. Only the question found it.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "What are my options here?" Fines, fees, penalties, deadlines — ask for the menu before accepting the worst item on it.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 7</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> accepting the outcome with the lesson attached. Complete it:<br><br>"Fair enough — ___ learned."</p>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"Fair enough — lesson learned."</b><br>Graceful acceptance, with the learning named — which tells the official his discretion landed on the right person. No relitigating, no "but really the app should…", no sourness. He bent a rule for you; the repayment is making him feel right about it, instantly. That feeling is what keeps discretion alive for the next honest passenger.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "Fair enough — lesson learned." Fines, dressing-downs, hard feedback — accept the outcome and the learning together.</div>
        </div>

        <h3>🗺️ Leaving smarter than you boarded</h3>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 8</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> fixing the cause while the expert watches. Complete it:<br><br>"Doing it right now while you ___ — there. Default zone saved."</p>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"Doing it right now while you watch — there. Default zone saved."</b><br>He told you the fix ("settings, then travel profile — everyone misses it"); doing it on the spot, in front of him, closes the whole story properly: the mistake is not just paid for but made unrepeatable, witnessed. It's the difference between apologising for a mistake and retiring it. Officials remember the passengers who fixed the thing. Fondly.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "Let me fix that right now while you're here." IT support, form corrections, any expert with thirty seconds and the answer.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 9</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> learning the appeal route you (hopefully) won't need. Complete it:<br><br>"If this had gone the other way — where would I have ___, just so I know the system?"</p>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"Where would I have appealed, just so I know the system?"</b><br>Asked AFTER everything is settled — which is why it lands as curiosity, not threat. Ask it mid-argument and it's a weapon; ask it at the end and it's a citizen learning the machinery, and the answer comes generously: fourteen days, back of the notice, include the history, "you'd have won, probably." Now you know the route for the day an inspector has no discretion — or no coffee.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "Just so I know the system — how would an appeal work?" Every penalty, every rejection, asked in peacetime.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 10</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> the human close, to a person whose day is mostly conflict. Say it out loud, then write it.</p>
            <textarea class="blank" placeholder="Twelve euros paid, app fixed, appeal route learned. He's moving down the carriage…"></textarea>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal a strong version</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>e.g. "Thanks for hearing me out — I imagine most of these conversations are worse."</b><br>His answer — "you have no idea" — is the whole job description of a ticket inspector: shouted at, lied to, filmed, daily. Thirty seconds of being treated like a reasonable human doing a needed job is rarer on that tram than a valid Zone D ticket. It costs nothing. And the transport system of your city is smaller than you think: inspectors remember faces both ways.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "Thanks for hearing me out — I imagine most of these go worse." For every official whose day is made of conflict.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="keep">
            <h3>🔑 Make them yours</h3>
            <p>These ten work wherever you're honestly in the wrong with an official attached. Say each answer out loud before you type it.</p>
            <p class="cue">"Before you check — I think I've done something wrong." — what's the mistake in your life right now that would cost less declared than discovered?</p>
            <textarea class="blank" placeholder="The thing to get ahead of…"></textarea>
            <p class="cue">"That's an explanation, not an excuse." — practise the label on a real recent mistake: give the context, then sort it out loud.</p>
            <textarea class="blank" placeholder="The context, correctly labelled…"></textarea>
            <p class="cue">"What are my options here?" — recall a penalty you paid in full. Was there a menu you never asked for?</p>
            <textarea class="blank" placeholder="The fine, and the question you didn't ask…"></textarea>
        </div>

        <a class="morelink" href="everyday_echoes_phrase_bank.html">🧰 <span>Every phrase from every Everyday Echoes lesson, sorted by what it does.</span><span class="go">Open →</span></a>

        <div class="tip"><b>Quick test:</b> the ninety seconds, out loud — before you scan it, something's wrong → my mistake, not the machine's → explanation, not excuse → here's the history, you can scroll → not dodging, I'll pay the difference → what are my options → fair enough, lesson learned → fixing it while you watch → where would I have appealed → thanks for hearing me out. Sixty euros of fine; ten sentences between you and twelve.</div>""",

