"""        <h2>The Echo <span class="em">🕰️</span></h2>

        <div class="echo-card">
            <p>Al Capone ran Chicago. Breweries, speakeasies, judges, aldermen, the St Valentine's Day massacre — everyone knew, and no one could prove a line of it. The most feared man in America was finally brought down by, of all things, paperwork: in 1931, the federal government convicted him not of murder or bootlegging but of tax evasion. Eleven years, Alcatraz, the empire gone — over the small official thing, not the big dramatic one.</p>
            <p>The lesson his lawyers understood too late has governed every encounter with officialdom since: systems can forgive almost anything except being lied to inside their own paperwork. Capone's actual crimes were beyond the government's reach; the false and missing filings were not. The small thing IS the big thing, to the system — because the small thing is the part it can check.</p>
            <p>Your wrong-zone ticket is not a crime empire, and Inspector Brandt is not the Treasury Department. But the mechanism on that tram is Capone's mechanism in miniature: the fare was never really the issue — the honesty about it was. Declare, own, pay the difference, and the system's whole machinery turns friendly. It's the ones who make the paperwork fight them who find out how heavy it is.</p>
        </div>

        <a class="morelink" href="short_history_al_capone.html">🕴️ <span>Want the full story? The empire, the accountants, and the taxes that did what bullets couldn't…</span><span class="go">Open →</span></a>

        <h3>Two bigger questions</h3>
        <ol class="bigq">
            <li>Capone fell to paperwork, not justice for his real crimes. Is that a triumph of the system or an indictment of it?</li>
            <li>"The system can forgive anything except being lied to inside its own paperwork." Where have you seen the honest route cost less than the clever one?</li>
        </ol>""",
